iPhone Duo is the first iPhone that can run two apps on the screen at the same time. Unfold the phone in landscape orientation, and Split View puts two apps side by side on the 7.6-inch inner display. It's especially handy for things like keeping Messages open next to Maps, or Safari next to Notes. Indeed, if there's an app combination that you use often, you can lock two together as an app pair, and they will always launch together. Here's how to do both.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/iphone-duo-split-screen-scaled-e1789143118947.jpg" alt="" width="2163" height="1212" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1064161" /><br/>
<h2>Open Two Apps in Split View</h2><br/>
The way it currently works, Split View can be invoked on the iPhone Duo's inner display in landscape orientation. Here are the two ways to start it.<br/>
<br/>
<h3>From an App You're Already Using:</h3><br/>
<ol><li>Swipe up from the bottom of the screen, pause in the center, then keep dragging the app toward the left or right edge. The app will settle into that half of the screen, and the Home Screen will appear on the other half.<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/split-view-home-screen-duo.jpeg" alt="kindle" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1072465" /><br/>
<li>Now select the second app you want to open. You can tap its icon from the Home Screen, swipe down to search for it, or swipe leftwards to find it in the App Library.</li><br/>
<li>Tap the second app, and it will appear alongside the first.<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/split-view-home-screen-duo-2.jpeg" alt="app store" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1072466" /></ol><br/>
<br/>
<h3>From the Home Screen or App Library:</h3><br/>
<br/>
<ol><li>Touch and hold an app icon, then tap <strong>Open in Split View</strong>.<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/split-view-app-home-screen.jpeg" alt="split view" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1072481" /><br/>
<li>Tap the second app you want to open alongside it from the Home Screen, or using swipe-down search or the App Library.<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/second-app-split-view.jpeg" alt="split view" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1072483" /></ol><br/>
<br/>
We've found that in certain cases, two windows of the same app also work. Apps that support multiple windows include Safari, Mail, Notes and Files. Simply open the app in Split View, tap its icon again on the Home Screen or in App Library, and a second window opens beside the first. This way, you can have two webpages open at once, or your inbox next to a draft. It's pretty neat.<br/>
<br/>
To swap the two apps around, just swipe up from the bottom of either app, pause in the center, then drag it to the opposite side.<br/>
<br/>
<h2>Lock Two Apps as an App Pair</h2><br/>
<ol><li>With both apps open in Split View, tap the divider between them.<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/lock-app-pair-duo.jpeg" alt="lock pair" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1072486" /><br/>
<li>There isn't really a second step – that's essentially it. The apps are now paired, and the next time you open either one, the other one will open with it automatically.<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/locked-app-pair-duo.jpeg" alt="locked pair" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1072487" /></ol><br/>
To unpair them at any time, just drag the divider in the center, all the way to the left or right edge of the screen. The app you push off will close, while the other will return to full screen.<br/>
<br/>
<h2>Worth Knowing</h2><br/>
Unlike in iPadOS, the app split on iPhone Duo is fixed at 50/50. So if you're used to Split View on iPad, remember that you can't drag the divider to give one app more room. Also, only one app can play audio at a time, so it's not like you can scroll two video feeds side by side and expect to get sound from both.<div class="linkback">Related Roundup: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a></div><div class="linkback">Buyer's Guide: <a href="https://buyersguide.macrumors.com/#iPhone-Duo">iPhone Duo (Buy Now)</a></div><div class="linkback">Related Forum: <a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/forums/iphone.99">iPhone</a></div><br/>This article, &quot;<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/how-to/iphone-duo-open-two-apps-side-by-side/">iPhone Duo: Open Two Apps Side by Side and Lock Them As an App Pair</a>&quot; first appeared on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com">MacRumors.com</a><br/><br/><a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/iphone-duo-open-two-apps-side-by-side-and-lock-them-as-an-app-pair.2492493/">Discuss this article</a> in our forums<br/><br/>